AI, Power, and Democratic Viability

Toward a Mathematical Theory of Technological Concentration and Democratic Stability

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Kimi 

Abstract

The rapid development of artificial intelligence transforms the relationships among labor, capital, information, and political power. This paper develops a unified dynamic framework in which AI-driven concentration of economic, informational, and coercive capabilities can destabilize democratic institutions. Building on the automation–repression complementarity of Acemoglu, Gitmez, and Shadmehr (2026), we extend the analysis to a multidimensional setting where AI simultaneously alters productive, informational, political, and coercive capacities.

The contribution is threefold. First, we construct a dynamic game with microfounded behavior by capital owners, workers, and the state. We explicitly model capital concentration as endogenous through the capital share, link informational AI to the precision of signals in a global game of revolt, and embed coordination AI in the critical mass required for collective action. Second, we introduce a timescale separation between fast variables (inequality, political power, coercive capacity, fiscal capacity) and slow variables (concentrated capital and institutional quality). Using singular perturbation theory, we reduce the six-dimensional system to a two-dimensional slow manifold, on which the democratic equilibrium’s stability is determined. The threshold condition is derived from the vanishing of the determinant of the slow Jacobian, which in the economically relevant limit reduces to the equality of the AI-induced concentration rate and the institutional adaptation rate. Third, we characterize the bifurcation at this threshold as a saddle-node under explicit nondegeneracy conditions, implying hysteresis and path dependence in democratic erosion.

The central result is that democratic stability requires institutional adaptation to outpace AI-induced concentration. When this condition fails, the system crosses the boundary of the Democratic Viability Region via a saddle-node bifurcation, transitioning irreversibly toward an Authoritarian Concentration Equilibrium. We conclude with empirical implications that require nonlinear methods capable of detecting thresholds and hysteresis.

---

1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence is more than another wave of industrial automation. Previous technological revolutions primarily transformed the productivity of physical labor. Contemporary AI systems increasingly transform cognitive labor, information processing, prediction, communication, and administration. This creates a new political-economic question: what happens to democratic institutions when the productive capacity of a society becomes concentrated in systems controlled by a small number of actors?

Recent formal political economy has shown that automation can generate endogenous pressures toward authoritarian governance. Acemoglu, Gitmez, and Shadmehr (2026) prove that automation creates a complementarity with repression: as automation reduces labor’s share of income, redistribution becomes costly relative to repression. Their dynamic extension shows that capital accumulation combined with automation encourages capitalists to support a coup against democracy.

This paper takes that insight as a foundation and extends it along three dimensions. First, we endogenize capital concentration by linking it to the capital share, which AI increases. Second, we explicitly model informational and coordination dimensions of AI—not merely productive and surveillance capabilities—by embedding them in a global game of revolt. Third, we derive the central stability condition from a rigorous bifurcation analysis of the full dynamical system using timescale separation.

The central proposition is:

High AI capability + Concentrated ownership + Weak institutional adaptation → Democratic fragility.

We formalize this proposition by constructing a dynamic game with three actors, deriving equilibrium conditions, and analyzing the stability of the resulting dynamical system.

1.1 Contribution Relative to Existing Literature

Relative to Acemoglu, Gitmez, and Shadmehr (2026), this paper contributes in three ways.

First, we make capital concentration endogenous. Instead of assuming a fixed share of capital income accruing to the top one percent, we show that the capital share itself—driven by AI—determines the income share of concentrated owners. This provides a microfoundation for the self-reinforcing concentration mechanism.

Second, we integrate informational and coordination AI into the stability analysis. In prior work, only productive and surveillance capabilities entered the political equilibrium. Here, informational AI ($A^i$) affects the variance of signals in a global game, and coordination AI ($A^c$) alters the critical mass required for revolt. We then embed the resulting revolt probability into the dynamics of coercive capacity, ensuring that these dimensions influence the system’s eigenvalues.

Third, we rigorously derive the bifurcation threshold using singular perturbation theory. We argue that inequality, political power, coercive capacity, and fiscal capacity adjust quickly relative to concentrated capital and institutional quality. This timescale separation justifies a reduction to a two-dimensional slow manifold, on which a saddle-node bifurcation can be cleanly analyzed. The threshold condition is obtained from the determinant of the slow Jacobian, which in the relevant limit reduces to the equality of the AI-induced concentration rate and the institutional adaptation rate.

1.2 Structure of the Paper

Section 2 reviews the theoretical foundations. Section 3 develops the dynamic game with endogenous capital concentration and informational/coordination AI. Section 4 defines the Democratic Viability Region. Section 5 performs the timescale separation and stability analysis, deriving the central threshold. Section 6 characterizes the equilibria and the saddle-node bifurcation. Section 7 discusses empirical implications. Section 8 concludes.

