4. The Democratic Viability Region

Definition 1 (Democratic Viability Region). The Democratic Viability Region $\mathcal{V} \subset \mathbb{R}^6$ is the set of states from which the democratic equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable. Formally,

\mathcal{V} = \{X \in \mathbb{R}^6 : \text{all eigenvalues of } J(X^*) \text{ have negative real parts}\},

where $J(X^*)$ is the Jacobian of the full system evaluated at the democratic equilibrium $X^*$.

The boundary $\partial\mathcal{V}$ is the set where at least one eigenvalue has zero real part—the bifurcation set.

---

5. Timescale Separation and Stability Analysis

5.1 Timescale Separation

We assume that the variables $I^{ineq}, P, R, F$ adjust quickly relative to $K$ and $D$. This is economically plausible: inequality, political power, coercive capacity, and fiscal capacity re-equilibrate on shorter timescales than aggregate capital concentration and institutional quality. Let $\varepsilon$ be a small parameter representing the ratio of fast to slow timescales. The fast subsystem is

\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon \dot I^{ineq} &= \lambda_K K - \lambda_T T, \\
\varepsilon \dot P &= \mu_K K + \mu_I I^{ineq} - \mu_D D, \\
\varepsilon \dot R &= \nu_A A^s + \nu_P P - \nu_D D + \nu_Q Q, \\
\varepsilon \dot F &= \psi_Y Y + \psi_D D F - \psi_K K F.
\end{aligned}

The slow subsystem is

\begin{aligned}
\dot K &= \kappa_0 s_K \frac{Y}{K} K - \delta_K K - \tau K, \\
\dot D &= \eta_D D (1-D)(L - \bar{L}) - \eta_P P D - \eta_R R D.
\end{aligned}

In the limit $\varepsilon \to 0$, the fast variables attain their quasi-equilibria as functions of $(K,D)$. By Fenichel’s theorem, for $\varepsilon$ sufficiently small, the full system possesses a normally hyperbolic slow manifold that is $O(\varepsilon)$-close to the critical manifold. The dynamics on this slow manifold are governed by the reduced two-dimensional system in $(K,D)$.

5.2 Reduced Slow Dynamics

Let the quasi-equilibrium values be denoted by $I^{ineq*}(K,D)$, $P^*(K,D)$, $R^*(K,D)$, and $F^*(K,D)$. Substituting into the slow equations yields the reduced system:

\begin{aligned}
\dot K &= f(K,D), \\
\dot D &= g(K,D),
\end{aligned}

where

f(K,D) = \kappa_0 s_K(K,D) \frac{Y(K,D)}{K} K - \delta_K K - \tau(K,D) K,

and

g(K,D) = \eta_D D (1-D)\bigl(L(K,D) - \bar{L}\bigr) - \eta_P P^*(K,D) D - \eta_R R^*(K,D) D.

The Jacobian of the reduced system is

J_{\text{slow}} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
f_K & f_D \\
g_K & g_D
\end{pmatrix},

where subscripts denote partial derivatives.

5.3 Stability Condition and Bifurcation Threshold

The democratic equilibrium $X^* = (K^*, D^*)$ of the reduced system is locally asymptotically stable if and only if both eigenvalues of $J_{\text{slow}}$ have negative real parts. A necessary and sufficient condition for a zero eigenvalue is

\det(J_{\text{slow}}) = f_K g_D - f_D g_K = 0.

We now interpret this condition economically. Define the AI-induced concentration rate as

\Gamma_{AI} = f_K = \frac{\partial \dot K}{\partial K}\bigg|_{X^*}.

This measures the marginal effect of capital accumulation on its own growth rate. Define the institutional adaptation rate as

\Gamma_{INST} = g_D = \frac{\partial \dot D}{\partial D}\bigg|_{X^*}.

In general, the threshold condition is $\det(J_{\text{slow}}) = 0$, which involves both diagonal and off-diagonal terms. However, if the cross-effects are small relative to the diagonal terms—that is, if $|f_D g_K| \ll |f_K g_D|$—then the condition reduces to $f_K g_D = 0$. Since in a democratic equilibrium we expect $g_D > 0$ (institutions adapt positively), the relevant bifurcation occurs when $f_K = 0$. Under the normalization that $\Gamma_{INST}$ is positive, the threshold is approximately

\boxed{\Gamma_{AI} = \Gamma_{INST}}

where we interpret $\Gamma_{INST}$ as the rate of institutional adaptation and $\Gamma_{AI}$ as the net rate of concentrated capital accumulation. This equality is the central threshold condition: democratic stability requires $\Gamma_{INST} > \Gamma_{AI}$.

5.4 Interpretation

$\Gamma_{AI}$ is the net growth rate of concentrated capital. It increases with AI because $s_K$ rises. $\Gamma_{INST}$ is the net rate at which democratic institutions strengthen. The threshold $\Gamma_{AI} = \Gamma_{INST}$ marks the point where the destabilizing force of economic concentration exactly balances the stabilizing force of institutional adaptation. When $\Gamma_{AI}$ exceeds $\Gamma_{INST}$, the democratic equilibrium loses stability through a zero eigenvalue.

---

6. Equilibrium Analysis and Bifurcation Structure

6.1 Three Long-Run Equilibria

· Democratic Abundance Equilibrium ($X_D^*$): $\Gamma_{INST} > \Gamma_{AI}$, stable, lies in $\mathcal{V}$.
· Authoritarian Concentration Equilibrium ($X_A^*$): $\Gamma_{AI} > \Gamma_{INST}$, repression cost low, institutions eroded.
· Technocratic Democracy Equilibrium ($X_T^*$): $\Gamma_{INST} \approx \Gamma_{AI}$, legitimate through procedural oversight.

6.2 Saddle-Node Bifurcation

Proposition 1 (Local Bifurcation). As the AI capability parameter $A$ increases, the democratic equilibrium $X_D^*$ undergoes a local bifurcation at $A = A^*$ where $\Gamma_{AI}(A^*) = \Gamma_{INST}(A^*)$. At $A = A^*$, an eigenvalue of the reduced slow Jacobian crosses zero.

Proof. By the threshold condition derived in Section 5.3, $\det(J_{\text{slow}}) = 0$ when $\Gamma_{AI} = \Gamma_{INST}$ in the limit of negligible cross-effects. The transversality condition $\frac{d}{dA}\det(J_{\text{slow}})\big|_{A=A^*} \neq 0$ is assumed to hold, ensuring the eigenvalue crosses with non-zero speed. ∎

Proposition 2 (Saddle-Node and Hysteresis). The bifurcation identified in Proposition 1 is a saddle-node if the following nondegeneracy conditions hold:

1. Quadratic nondegeneracy: On the one-dimensional center manifold, the reduced dynamics have a non-zero quadratic term. This is satisfied if the second derivative of the normal form does not vanish.
2. Transversality: $\frac{\partial \lambda_{\max}}{\partial A}\big|_{A=A^*} \neq 0$, where $\lambda_{\max}$ is the dominant eigenvalue.

Under these conditions, the stable democratic equilibrium and a saddle-type authoritarian equilibrium annihilate each other, implying hysteresis. Once the system transitions to the authoritarian equilibrium, reversing the transition requires reducing $A$ below a lower threshold $A^{**} < A^*$.

Proof Sketch. By the Center Manifold Theorem, the dynamics on the one-dimensional center manifold can be written in the normal form $\dot x = a x^2 + b \mu$, where $\mu = A - A^*$. The conditions ensure $a \neq 0$ and $b \neq 0$. The bifurcation diagram is a fold, creating hysteresis. ∎

Corollary 1 (Path Dependence). Democratic erosion is path-dependent: prevention is easier than reversal. The threshold for returning to democracy ($A^{**}$) is strictly lower than the threshold for its initial erosion ($A^*$).

