7. Empirical Implications

7.1 Testable Hypotheses

· H1 (Concentration Effect): Higher AI exposure is positively correlated with capital concentration.
· H3 (Institutional Mediation): The effect of AI on democratic instability is stronger when redistributive capacity is weak.
· H4 (Repression Complementarity): Political repression becomes more attractive as capital concentration and instability rise.
· H5 (Nonlinearity and Threshold): There exists a threshold $A^*$ such that the marginal effect of AI on democratic fragility is small for $A < A^*$ and large for $A > A^*$.
· H7 (Hysteresis): Once democratic erosion occurs, recovery requires reducing AI concentration significantly below the original threshold.

7.2 Identification Strategy

A shift-share instrument based on industry-level AI adoption trends in other countries, interacted with local industry composition, addresses endogeneity. To test H5 and H7, we require nonlinear methods: threshold regression (Hansen, 1999) to estimate the break point, regime-switching models to detect transitions, and longitudinal case studies to identify hysteresis.

The core specification is

\text{Democratic Erosion}_{i,t+1} = \beta_1 \text{AI Exposure}_{i,t} \cdot \mathbb{1}(\text{AI Exposure}_{i,t} \leq A^*) + \beta_2 \text{AI Exposure}_{i,t} \cdot \mathbb{1}(\text{AI Exposure}_{i,t} > A^*) + \gamma' X_{i,t} + \alpha_i + \delta_t + \epsilon_{i,t},

where the prediction is $\beta_2 > \beta_1$.

7.3 Falsification Conditions

The theory is falsified if no threshold is observed, hysteresis is absent, or informational/coordination AI have no measurable political consequences.

---

8. Conclusion

Artificial intelligence is not inherently democratic or authoritarian. It is a force that can change the distribution of productive, informational, political, and coercive power. The fundamental question is: who controls the power created by AI?

This paper has developed a formal framework to analyze that question. The key contribution is the rigorous derivation of a threshold condition—$\Gamma_{INST} > \Gamma_{AI}$—from the stability properties of a microfounded dynamic system with timescale separation. We have shown that crossing this threshold corresponds to a saddle-node bifurcation, implying hysteresis and the irreversibility of democratic erosion.

The decisive variable is not AI capability itself, but the relationship between technological concentration and institutional adaptation. The central research challenge is to measure this relationship empirically and to determine whether democratic institutions can keep advanced technological societies within a viable democratic state space.

