Appendix B: Global Game of Revolt and AI Channels

B.1 Setup

Workers observe private signals about the regime’s strength $\theta_t$:

x_j = \theta_t + \epsilon_j, \qquad \epsilon_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\epsilon^2(A^i)),

where $\sigma_\epsilon^2$ is increasing in informational AI $A^i$. A revolt succeeds if the fraction of workers participating exceeds a critical mass $\bar{q}(A^c) \in (0,1)$. Coordination AI $A^c$ can raise $\bar{q}$ if used by the state for counter-organization.

B.2 Threshold Equilibrium

We focus on symmetric threshold strategies: worker $j$ participates iff $x_j \le x^*$. The fraction of participants at state $\theta$ is

\Pr(x_j \le x^* \mid \theta) = \Phi\left( \frac{x^* - \theta}{\sigma_\epsilon} \right).

The revolt succeeds iff

\Phi\left( \frac{x^* - \theta}{\sigma_\epsilon} \right) \ge \bar{q}(A^c),

which defines the critical state $\theta^*$ by

\theta^* = x^* - \sigma_\epsilon \Phi^{-1}(\bar{q}(A^c)).

A worker with signal $x^*$ must be indifferent between participating and not. The expected payoff from participating is $B \Pr(\theta \le \theta^* \mid x^*) - c \Pr(\theta > \theta^* \mid x^*)$, where $B>0$ is the benefit of a successful revolt and $c>0$ the cost of participation in a failed one. Indifference requires

\Pr(\theta \le \theta^* \mid x^*) = \frac{c}{B+c} \equiv p^*.

Given the prior $\theta \sim \mathcal{N}(\bar{\theta}, \sigma_\theta^2)$, the posterior distribution of $\theta$ conditional on $x^*$ is normal with mean and variance given by standard Bayesian updating. In the limit of a diffuse prior ($\sigma_\theta^2 \to \infty$), the posterior mean converges to $x^*$ and the posterior variance to $\sigma_\epsilon^2$. The indifference condition then yields

\Phi\left( \frac{\theta^* - x^*}{\sigma_\epsilon} \right) = p^*.

Combining with the definition of $\theta^*$ gives the equilibrium condition $\bar{q} = p^*$, which is independent of signal noise. For finite priors, the equilibrium is more involved, but the qualitative effect of increased $\sigma_\epsilon$ is to reduce the ex ante probability of successful coordination.

Proposition B.1 (Revolt probability).
The ex ante probability of a successful revolt is

Q_t \equiv \Pr(\theta \le \theta^*) = \Phi\left( \frac{\theta^* - \bar{\theta}}{\sigma_\theta} \right).

Proof. Immediate from the definition of $\theta^*$ and the prior distribution of $\theta$. ∎

Proposition B.2 (Informational AI reduces revolt probability).
An increase in $A^i$, which increases $\sigma_\epsilon$, reduces $Q_t$.

Proof. In the global game, higher signal noise makes workers’ signals less correlated, reducing the ability to coordinate on a common threshold. Formally, the equilibrium threshold $\theta^*$ decreases with $\sigma_\epsilon$ (see Morris and Shin (2003) for the general argument), and $Q_t$ is increasing in $\theta^*$. ∎

Proposition B.3 (Coordination AI and critical mass).
If state-controlled $A^c$ raises $\bar{q}$, then $Q_t$ falls.

Proof. A higher $\bar{q}$ increases $\theta^*$ (from the defining equation), which lowers $Q_t$. ∎

