Appendix C: Repression–Redistribution Trade-off

C.1 Redistribution Cost

To achieve worker welfare $\bar{w}$, the required tax rate on capital solves

\bar{w} = w_t + \tau_t \frac{s_{K,t} Y_t}{L_t},

so

\tau_t^* = \frac{(\bar{w} - w_t) L_t}{s_{K,t} Y_t}.

The total cost of redistribution is

C_R(\tau_t, I_t, Y_t) = \tau_t s_{K,t} Y_t + \psi \tau_t^2 Y_t,

where the second term captures deadweight loss, $\psi > 0$.

Proposition C.1 (Automation raises redistribution cost).
Holding worker welfare constant, $\frac{\partial C_R}{\partial I_t} > 0$.

Proof. As $I_t$ rises, $s_{K,t}$ falls (Proposition A.1), requiring a higher $\tau_t$ to maintain the same transfer. The deadweight loss term is convex in $\tau_t$, so total cost increases. ∎

C.2 Repression Cost

Repression cost is

C_P(R_t, Q_t, A^s_t) = \frac{c_P Q_t}{R_t + A^s_t},

with $c_P > 0$, $R_t$ coercive capacity, $Q_t$ revolt probability, and $A^s_t$ surveillance AI.

Proposition C.2 (Surveillance and informational AI reduce repression cost).
$\frac{\partial C_P}{\partial A^s} < 0$ and $\frac{\partial C_P}{\partial A^i} < 0$.

Proof. Direct differentiation: $\partial C_P/\partial A^s = -c_P Q_t / (R_t + A^s_t)^2 < 0$. Since $\partial Q_t/\partial A^i < 0$ (Proposition B.2), $\partial C_P/\partial A^i = (c_P/(R_t + A^s_t)) \partial Q_t/\partial A^i < 0$. ∎

C.3 Choice Between Repression and Redistribution

Define $\Omega_t = C_R/C_P$. The state chooses repression if $\Omega_t > 1$.

Proposition C.3 (AI increases repression attractiveness).
$\frac{\partial \Omega_t}{\partial A^p} > 0$, $\frac{\partial \Omega_t}{\partial A^i} > 0$, $\frac{\partial \Omega_t}{\partial A^s} > 0$.

Proof. Each AI dimension either raises $C_R$ or lowers $C_P$, as shown above. ∎

