Appendix D: Full Dynamical System and Jacobian

D.1 State Variables and Equations

The state vector is $X_t = (K_t, I_t^{ineq}, P_t, R_t, D_t, F_t) \in \mathbb{R}^6$, where:

· $K_t$: capital stock owned by concentrated owners.
· $I_t^{ineq}$: inequality (e.g., Gini coefficient) driven by automation.
· $P_t$: political power concentration.
· $R_t$: coercive capacity.
· $D_t$: institutional quality (democratic).
· $F_t$: fiscal capacity.

The evolution equations are:

\begin{aligned}
\dot K_t &= \kappa_0 s_{K,t} Y_t - (\delta_K + \tau_t) K_t, \\
\dot I_t^{ineq} &= \lambda_K K_t - \lambda_T T_t, \\
\dot P_t &= \mu_K K_t + \mu_I I_t^{ineq} - \mu_P P_t - \mu_D D_t, \\
\dot R_t &= \nu_A A^s_t + \nu_P P_t - \nu_R R_t - \nu_D D_t + \nu_Q Q_t(A^i, A^c), \\
\dot D_t &= \eta_D D_t (1 - D_t) (L_t - \bar{L}) - \eta_P P_t D_t - \eta_R R_t D_t, \\
\dot F_t &= \psi_Y Y_t + \psi_D D_t F_t - \psi_K K_t F_t.
\end{aligned}

Here $s_{K,t} = s_K(I_t)$ is increasing in automation; $Q_t = Q(A^i, A^c)$ as derived in Appendix B; $L_t = L(I_t^{ineq}, U_t, T_t)$ is legitimacy with $\partial L/\partial I^{ineq} < 0$, $\partial L/\partial U < 0$, $\partial L/\partial T > 0$. All Greek parameters are positive constants except where noted.

D.2 Dependence on Fast vs. Slow Variables

We assume the following economic timescales:

· Slow variables: $K_t$ (capital accumulation) and $D_t$ (institutional evolution).
· Fast variables: $I_t^{ineq}, P_t, R_t, F_t$, which adjust quickly to their quasi-equilibria.

This assumption is justified by empirical observation that inequality, political power, coercive capacity, and fiscal capacity re-equilibrate on shorter horizons than aggregate capital concentration or institutional quality (which may take decades). The formal treatment uses singular perturbation theory.

---

Appendix E: Timescale Separation and Slow Manifold Reduction

E.1 Singular Perturbation Formulation

Introduce a small parameter $\varepsilon > 0$ representing the ratio of fast to slow timescales. The system becomes:

\begin{aligned}
\dot K &= f(K, D, I^{ineq}, P, R, F), \\
\dot D &= g(K, D, I^{ineq}, P, R, F), \\
\varepsilon \dot I^{ineq} &= h_1(K, I^{ineq}), \\
\varepsilon \dot P &= h_2(K, I^{ineq}, P, D), \\
\varepsilon \dot R &= h_3(P, R, D, A^s, Q), \\
\varepsilon \dot F &= h_4(K, D, F).
\end{aligned}

In the limit $\varepsilon \to 0$, the fast variables converge to their quasi-equilibria defined by

0 = h_1(K, I^{ineq*}), \quad 0 = h_2(K, I^{ineq*}, P^*, D), \quad 0 = h_3(P^*, R^*, D, A^s, Q), \quad 0 = h_4(K, D, F^*).

This defines the critical manifold $\mathcal{M}_0 \subset \mathbb{R}^6$.

E.2 Normal Hyperbolicity and Fenichel's Theorem

Assumption E.1 (Normal Hyperbolicity). The Jacobian of the fast subsystem with respect to the fast variables,

J_{fast} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial h_1}{\partial I^{ineq}} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
\frac{\partial h_2}{\partial I^{ineq}} & \frac{\partial h_2}{\partial P} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \frac{\partial h_3}{\partial P} & \frac{\partial h_3}{\partial R} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & \frac{\partial h_4}{\partial F}
\end{pmatrix},

has eigenvalues with strictly negative real parts bounded away from zero on $\mathcal{M}_0$. This requires $\partial h_1/\partial I^{ineq} < 0$, $\partial h_2/\partial P < 0$, $\partial h_3/\partial R < 0$, $\partial h_4/\partial F < 0$, which we assume.

Under this assumption, Fenichel's theorem guarantees the existence of a slow manifold $\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$ that is $O(\varepsilon)$-close to $\mathcal{M}_0$ and invariant under the full flow. On $\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon$, the dynamics are $O(\varepsilon)$-close to the reduced dynamics obtained by substituting the quasi-equilibria into the slow equations.

E.3 Reduced Slow Dynamics

Let the quasi-equilibria be denoted by $I^{ineq*}(K)$, $P^*(K,D)$, $R^*(K,D)$, $F^*(K,D)$. The reduced slow system is:

\begin{aligned}
\dot K &= F(K,D) = f(K, D, I^{ineq*}(K), P^*(K,D), R^*(K,D), F^*(K,D)), \\
\dot D &= G(K,D) = g(K, D, I^{ineq*}(K), P^*(K,D), R^*(K,D), F^*(K,D)).
\end{aligned}

The reduced Jacobian is

J_{slow} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
F_K & F_D \\
G_K & G_D
\end{pmatrix},

evaluated at the democratic equilibrium $(K^*, D^*)$.

---

Appendix F: Bifurcation Analysis: Saddle-Node and Hysteresis

F.1 Zero Eigenvalue Condition

The democratic equilibrium loses stability when an eigenvalue of $J_{slow}$ crosses zero. A necessary condition is

\det(J_{slow}) = F_K G_D - F_D G_K = 0.

Definition F.1 (AI-induced concentration rate).

\Gamma_{AI} = F_K = \frac{\partial \dot K}{\partial K}\bigg|_{(K^*,D^*)}.

Definition F.2 (Institutional adaptation rate).

\Gamma_{INST} = G_D = \frac{\partial \dot D}{\partial D}\bigg|_{(K^*,D^*)}.

In the economically relevant case where the cross-effects are small relative to the diagonal terms—i.e., $|F_D G_K| \ll |F_K G_D|$—the determinant condition reduces to $F_K = 0$ or $G_D = 0$. At a stable democratic equilibrium we have $G_D > 0$ (institutions adapt), so the bifurcation is driven by $F_K = 0$. We interpret the threshold as

\boxed{\Gamma_{AI} = \Gamma_{INST}}

with the understanding that $\Gamma_{INST}$ is normalized to the level that exactly offsets the destabilizing concentration pressure. In the limiting case $F_D = G_K = 0$, the condition is exactly $\Gamma_{AI} = 0$ versus $\Gamma_{INST} > 0$.

F.2 Saddle-Node Bifurcation

Let $A$ denote a composite AI capability parameter that shifts $F$ and $G$.

Proposition F.1 (Local bifurcation).
There exists $A^*$ such that at $A = A^*$, the reduced Jacobian $J_{slow}$ has a zero eigenvalue. This occurs when $\Gamma_{AI}(A^*) = \Gamma_{INST}(A^*)$ (in the approximate sense above).

Proof. By continuity of $F_K$ and $G_D$ in $A$, and the assumption that $F_K$ crosses zero at some $A^*$ while $G_D > 0$, the determinant vanishes. Transversality ensures the eigenvalue crosses with nonzero speed. ∎

Proposition F.2 (Saddle-node and hysteresis).
Under the following nondegeneracy conditions, the bifurcation is a saddle-node:

1. Transversality: $\frac{d}{dA} \det(J_{slow})\big|_{A=A^*} \neq 0$.
2. Quadratic nondegeneracy: On the one-dimensional center manifold, the reduced dynamics have the normal form $\dot x = a x^2 + b \mu + O(x^3)$, with $a \neq 0$ and $b \neq 0$, where $\mu = A - A^*$.

Then the stable democratic equilibrium and a saddle-type authoritarian equilibrium annihilate at $A^*$, creating hysteresis: reversing the transition requires reducing $A$ below a lower threshold $A^{**} < A^*$.

Proof. By the Center Manifold Theorem, the 2D slow system can be reduced to a 1D system on the center manifold at $A = A^*$. The nondegeneracy conditions ensure the normal form is that of a saddle-node (fold) bifurcation. The bifurcation diagram is a fold, implying hysteresis. ∎

---

Appendix G: Verification of Transversality and Quadratic Nondegeneracy

We now provide explicit formulas showing that the nondegeneracy conditions are generically satisfied in our model.

G.1 Transversality

At the bifurcation point, assume $F_K = 0$ and $F_D = 0$ (cross-effect negligible). Then

J_{slow} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ G_K & G_D \end{pmatrix}.

The right eigenvector for the zero eigenvalue is $v = (1, -G_K/G_D)^T$, and the left eigenvector is $w = (1, 0)^T$. The transversality coefficient is

b = w^T \frac{\partial}{\partial A} \begin{pmatrix} F \\ G \end{pmatrix} = \frac{\partial F}{\partial A}.

Now $\partial F/\partial A$ captures the direct effect of AI on capital accumulation. Since $F = \kappa_0 s_K(I^*(K)) Y - (\delta_K+\tau)K$, and $A$ increases $s_K$, we have $\partial F/\partial A > 0$. Hence $b \neq 0$.

G.2 Quadratic Nondegeneracy

The quadratic coefficient is

a = \frac{1}{2} w^T \left. \frac{\partial^2}{\partial x^2} \begin{pmatrix} F \\ G \end{pmatrix} \right|_{X^*} (v, v).

With $v = (1, -G_K/G_D)^T$ and $w = (1,0)$, this simplifies to

a = \frac{1}{2} \left( F_{KK} - \frac{G_K}{G_D} F_{KD} \right).

Since $F_D = 0$ in our approximation, $F_{KD} = 0$, so $a = \frac{1}{2} F_{KK}$. We have

F_K = \kappa_0 \left( s_K' I_K^* Y + s_K Y_K \right) - (\delta_K + \tau).

Differentiating:

F_{KK} = \kappa_0 \left( s_K'' (I_K^*)^2 Y + s_K' I_{KK}^* Y + 2 s_K' I_K^* Y_K + s_K Y_{KK} \right).

Given diminishing returns ($Y_{KK} < 0$) and plausible assumptions on $I_K^*$, generically $F_{KK} \neq 0$. Therefore $a \neq 0$, satisfying quadratic nondegeneracy.

